[38;5;231m⏺[39m One last constraint question before I work up approaches — it bounds what's even viable. Right now [38;5;153mindex.html[39m loads
  [38;5;153mapp.js[39m with a bare [38;5;153m<script src="app.js">[39m: no modules, no bundler, no build step, and [38;5;153mlogin[39m/[38;5;153mvalidateForm[39m are plain
  globals. A shared identity layer that "works across the app" needs [3msome[0m way for other files to reach it, and the
  options differ by whether you're willing to change that setup.

  Staying no-build means the identity layer is another plain script tag exposing a global (or a namespaced object) —
  zero tooling, works immediately, but relies on script ordering and global namespace discipline, which gets fragile as
  more forms arrive. Switching to ES modules ([38;5;153m<script type="module">[39m) gives real imports and explicit dependencies with
  no bundler needed, costs you [38;5;153mfile://[39m usage (modules need a server due to CORS) and is a small change to [38;5;153mindex.html[39m now
  rather than a painful one across many files later. A bundler is almost certainly overkill for this.

  Since you said other forms will need this later, the import story is the thing that gets expensive to change once
  there are several consumers.
